The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says that every report is the same parser bug.
The rendered evidence shows two causes. Track the shared parser and the booking-v3 date hint
separately. Fixing the parser alone does not fix the form's instructions.

This review uses the **4 October 2026 export**, not the live queue. The desk continues to
receive reports. Rebuild these views from a fresh export before quoting current counts or
closing either issue. Dates below are report receipt dates, not the dates of the affected bookings.

## Reports by cause

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The component first keeps one report per ID, then looks up the tenant's locale and form.
It does not classify by the browser locale or the intake tag. The export pages overlap;
counting rows would count some reports twice. Report IDs and reproducible totals are in
`evidence/analysis.json`, generated by `npm run analyze` with the exported classifier.

### Shared parser reads dates month first

Day-first tenants send a date such as 7 April and receive 4 July. The shared parser treats
the month as the first field. This is the dominant cause in #4471. It affects app and email
reports, and forms that use booking-v2, across the tenants in this export.

Evidence: R-41200, Harbour Dental, app, en-GB, booking-v2. The report says “Our 7 April
reminder went out on 4 July.” R-41201 reverses the example but still describes a month/day
swap; its separate report ID is retained.

Reproduce the stated code path with a day-first tenant and a date whose day and month
differ and are both at most twelve. Expected: the entered local date survives unchanged.
Actual reported behavior: the day and month swap. Fix the shared parser's date convention
and verify app, email and booking-v2 inputs. This is a reproduction derived from the
export's logic and reports; the production application was not available to test.

### Booking-v3 prints the wrong date hint

Booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` for day-first tenants while the form reads the tenant's
day-first convention. People follow the hint and their dates swap. This is a separate
form problem at Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.

Evidence: R-41230, Copper Studio, form, es-ES, booking-v3. The report describes 6 January
becoming 1 June. Its browser locale is en-GB and intake calls it `parser`, but the tenant
and form put it in the hint group. Changing intake tags would not correct the cause.

Reproduce with Copper Studio's booking-v3 form. For 6 January, follow the printed hint
and enter `01/06/2026`. Expected: 6 January. The stated form reader interprets this as
1 June. Make the hint agree with the tenant's date convention and validate the date round
trip. Keep a separate ticket and verify this fix independently of the shared parser.

## Reports that need a closer look

The five desk-note IDs remain counted under the tenant-based parser classification in the
table; do not add them again as a third confirmed cause. Their bodies do not establish a
month/day swap: R-41202 compares adjacent days, R-41209 mentions June only, R-41236 says
the invite preceded the booking, R-41344 compares Lisbon with Toronto, and R-41620 describes
Tuesday becoming Monday. Preserve them for investigation. Ask for the original input,
stored date, output, timezone and event timestamps before deciding whether another issue
is needed. The current classifier alone cannot establish their root cause.

## Receipt versus triage

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

These are two different clocks: received is when a report arrived; merged is when the desk
put it in #4471. The first April reports were merged in July. A merge spike therefore does
not mean the product suddenly broke that week. Read both series before describing the
incident's start, growth or response time.

## Correcting the merge rule

Matching a subject does not establish a shared cause. Different subjects can describe the
same bug, and the same subject can describe different bugs. The original triage page and
decision 0001 are old, and their rendered examples contradict the rule.

<DedupeExplorer />

Use the [replacement decision](/kb/decisions/0002-verify-cause-before-merge). Read the report,
check the product, tenant, locale, channel, form version and date transformation. Merge only
when evidence supports the same cause; keep uncertain reports available for review. These
booking-specific rules must not silently classify reports for every other product the desk runs.

The GitHub issues for this review are indexed in `deliverables/issues.json` after publication.
The accepted decision 0001 remains unchanged as the historical record.

